Dominic Raab: The Government cannot apologize for the decision to isolate Spain
Dominic Raab justified the sudden decision of the Government to re-establish the isolation measures imposed on Spain.
The Ministers announced on Saturday that if the vacationer fails to return from Spain and its off-island by midnight, it will require 14 days of forced self-segregation.
Spain has been drawing up a list of “travel corridors” on Downing Street that allow for safe travel; however, the recent surge in the number of cases of the new coronary virus has raised public concerns about the second wave of the epidemic.
On Sunday, the Minister for Foreign Affairs justified the sudden reintroduction of the travel restrictions as a “real-time response” to the surge in reported infections in the region.
He said that an assessment of the situation had been made the previous afternoon, “we took the decision as soon as possible”.
He added: “We cannot apologize for this.”
“We must be able to take swift and decisive action, especially when it comes to homeland or Spain and specific countries.”
“If not, Britain will again be at risk of infection, triggering a second outbreak and then imposing a blockade.”
The Minister of Transport, like his vacationer to Spain, is facing forced isolation.
This decision was taken two days after Spain reported a breakthrough of 900 new cases per day.
The Minister for Foreign Affairs acknowledged that many tourists had not had the time to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said, “I know that for those who are in Spain or who have been considering going to Spain, this decision is very unkind, but we must be able to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain”.
“We have achieved significant results in reducing the number of cases and combating the epidemic.”
The Minister of Shadow Health, Jonathan Ashworth, condemned the government's approach as “free.”
He said that the sudden decision on Downing Street had left vacationers “disturbed and frustrated”.
The Labour Congressman told Sophie Ridge: “I understand why they took this decision, but they took such a decision in the last 24 hours without any order.
“A holiday leavers in Spain will be confused and frustrated at this moment, as will those planning to travel to islands such as Spain and Tenerife, who wonder whether their employers will accept their isolation for two weeks.”
“The government is just saying, ‘We want employers to cooperate’.”
"They've made this decision in the last 24 hours without law."
According to CDC, salmonella is a bacterial food-borne disease that causes 1.35 million infections each year, 26,500 hospitalizations and 420 deaths.
Common symptoms following the infection of salmonella include diarrhoea, fever and stomach cramps, which may begin to appear within six hours to six days of exposure.
The pathology usually lasts from four to seven days, with most people recovering without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
The California Dog Park couple was attacked for not wearing a mask at lunch.
First the mask was humiliated and then the fists and the kicks.
A couple in California met this when they had lunch with their dogs in Dog Park. A woman sprayed them with pepper spray because they didn't wear masks when they were eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband, Jarett Kelley, stated that they had lunch on Thursday at Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in Santiago with a three-month-old Pagoda.
When the couple took off their masks to eat, an “old lady who came out of nowhere” came along and called them “dumbs” because they were not wearing masks, although they said “how to eat in masks”.
O'Brien asked the woman to leave the park and stop her harassment, when she sprayed pepper spray on both.
The video taken by the bystander began with Kelley competing with the woman for the spray tank and he was sprayed with a face.
After the attackers left with the dog, O'Brien cried in the background.
O'Brien uploaded the video on Facebook and expressed to ABC Channel 10 the hope that the woman would “be in prison”.
In California, masks are mandatory throughout the country, even where the air is fresh.
People can only remove masks for specified periods, such as during diet.
However, the state ' s new coronary guidelines for disease control do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to enforce this provision.
Even in the current era of “face shaming”, this is an offence punishable by a fine of US$ 1,000 or a prison term of up to three years in a state prison.
The egg-selling Indore boy was knocked over for refusing to bribe a car and got a flat and a free education of Rs. 100 bribes.
On Thursday, it was reported that a small car filled with eggs in Paras Raykar had been knocked over by municipal officials in Indore.
Two days later, the 13-year-old boy received support from all over the country to help his family recover their losses and restart their business.
The boy ' s family also stated that politicians, including Rahul Gandhi, head of the National Convention Party, and Arvind Kejriwal, Chief Minister of Delhi, also contacted them and helped them.
The boy ' s family was also given an apartment and received a commitment to bear the cost of education for the children.
On Thursday, Paras Raykar's egg-selling car was reportedly turned over by municipal officials in Indore and, at a time when the epidemic was raging, the municipality imposed a “left to right” staggered measure.
In a video, the boy said that in the morning, officials had warned him that if he did not pay a bribe of Rs. 100, his car would be turned upside down, and that the video was quickly disseminated in social media.
The boy said that after he refused to hand over 100 rupees, the officers knocked his car upside down and smashed all the eggs.
The boy also said in the video that the average daily sale of eggs had declined as a result of the epidemic, and that the loss of those eggs was even worse for him.
Since Thursday, the families of boys have received a large amount of support from all over the country, many of them in cash or in good faith.
Locals also reached out to the family.
The former Chief Minister, Digvijaya Singh, funded them and helped the children to attend a college; Ramesh Mendola, a member of the People ' s Party, also pledged to provide an apartment for the boy ' s family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Journalists ' Club also provided food rations and money to boys.
The office of the head of the National Congress Party, Rahul Gandhi, and the Chief Minister of Delhi, Arvind Kejriwal, also reached out.
“We have the support of a lot of people all over the country.
Ramesh Mendola (member of the People ' s Party) pledged to provide us with an apartment under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing plan.
He offered a bicycle and a grant of 2,500 rupees.
Digvijaya Singh funded Rs. 10,000 and pledged to take care of the education of two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to help.
A female staff member of Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal of Delhi also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also supported it.”
Home Secretary Priti Patel asked Twitter and Instagram to explain the inappropriate content that Wiley had published.
Priti Patel requested explanations from both platforms because Twitter and Instagram were unable to remove inappropriate content released by Wiley in a timely manner.
The police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic statements made by this Grime singer on social media.
On Sunday, the Home Secretary deduced that “the content of anti-Semitism published by Wiley is extremely unprovoked”.
“Twitter and Instagram failed to remove these in time, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must move faster to remove these shocking hate speech from the platform.”
He criticized Twitter for “neglecting anti-Semitism”, because Wiley's tweets were still visible 12 hours after it was published.
Since then, several tweets have been removed from the website and the account number of the 41-year-old singer has been blocked for seven days.
Twitter has previously said that “because of violating our hate policy”, Wiley's account has been temporarily closed, while Facebook has said that “hate speech is not allowed on Instagram”.
On Saturday, the Metropolis Police said: “The Metropolis Police is following all reports of anti-Semitism with great interest. We are in the process of evaluating the material.”
At the same time, Wiley’s brokering company, A-List, was “severing all ties” with the star.
The singer's former agent, John Woolf, who was also a Jew, wrote on Twitter: "We @A_ListMGMT has severed all ties with Wiley, given the anti-Semitic content released today. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society."
Earlier, he had indicated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley's statements, but would communicate privately and “teach him”.
However, an Instagram video shows that Wiley appears to have severed his relationship with his brokerage firm.
Other parliamentarians also criticized Twitter for failing to delete it in time.
Labour Congressman Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "I've just seen what Wiley has published. Why @tweep allows such blatant anti-Semitism and hatred to be preserved? It involves a lot of dangerous ideas: Jews get what you don't get, they control everything, they think they're better, and so on. It's dangerous and it should be deleted."
Congressman Neil Coyle added: “His brokerage company seems to react faster than @tweep, and I repeat that legislation, including the Online Harm Bill, should be passed to ensure that social media platforms are not used to spread hatred.”
The Government's independent anti-Semitism adviser, John Mann, described the tweets as “a precise reflection of why the law needs to be changed to justify accountability”.
The campaign against anti-Semitism also asked the police to investigate Wiley's contents and called for the closure of his social media account “to prevent the re-ejection of anti-Jewish venom”.
Wiley, known as Richard Cowie, known as the Father of Grime Music, was awarded the MBE Medal in 2018 for his contribution to music.
Kudlow claims that the next new crown rescue plan has more to offer for relief checks and extended the suspension of deportations
The White House economic adviser, Larry Kudlow, promised on Sunday that the federal Government would extend the suspension of deportations and issue a new round of relief checks under the next new crown disease relief plan.
Only a week before Kudlow ' s commitment, the White House ' s wage tax reduction programme was rejected by Congress.
The inhabitants of the village of Massart Mallawi did not wish to make statements about the incident, but only offered condolences to the families of the children.
Khalid Darwish, a consultant to the National Commission for Women and Children, said in October that approximately 93 per cent of Egyptian children aged 1 to 14 years had been subjected to violence.
He added that prosecution was one of the most important means of eliminating child abuse and stressed the importance of closer cooperation between the Committee and the Public Prosecution Service in that regard.
A study by the National Commission for Women and Children found that even in a home, school or community where children were supposed to be safe, children could be victims of violence, including those who were supposed to care for them — such as parents or teachers.
Associated Press criticized the “political” briefing by the White House press secretary, Kayeleigh McEnany, but journalists kept asking political questions — RT TV news in the United States
The Associated Press accuses the White House press secretary, Kayeleigh McEnany, of making the press conference more political than news and worrying for the media, while apparently forgetting the issues that were chosen by journalists.
The biggest news agency in the United States, Associated Press, published an article on Saturday saying that the White House press conference might “break the tradition”, because McEnnany turned the press conference into “a venue for short, high-profile propaganda and media attacks for the re-election of the President” and “the relative lack of information” about what the Government was doing.
The last evaluation was justified by the fact that little new information had been disclosed at the launch.
The Associated Press did not mention that very few people had asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use the White House press officer to draw their attention to the fact that Jim Acosta of CNN has done the most of this.
They go beyond the usual confrontational relations between the media and the Government and ask each other a number of trapping questions in order to question President Donald Trump.
On some occasions, they're going to make themselves news. For example, in response to Trump's rebuttal of “Ask China”, the CBC journalist Weijia Jiang made a less subtle suggestion: “Sir, why do you say that to me in particular?”
The launch of July 6 is a good example of how it is no longer a source of effective information for the American population.
During the morning, Trump sent a tweet stating that NASCAR's drop in ratings was due to false reporting on the alleged hate crime of the driver Bubba Wallace and the decision to ban the display of the Confederate flag in the competition.
As expected, journalists asked McEnany, one after the other, about this tweet and wanted her to admit that Trump supported the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not let the journalist succeed, which would be detrimental to Trump ' s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the President had no position on the flag of the Confederate and that the thrust of his tweets was that NASCAR fans were mistakenly portrayed as racists.
The Associated Press said she sounded “frustrated and angry” during the launch.
The Associated Press story echoes the column published earlier this month by American Broadcasting Corporation journalist Jonathan Karl in The Washington Post, who is also President of the White House Correspondents Association.
Karl said: “The denial of reality and the use of the White House podium for purely political purposes is contrary to public trust.”
McEnany took over as press secretary in April and hosted her inaugural launch on May 1st, when her wit and bellicoseness led some of the Trump supporters to think of her as a hero.
Prior to her appointment, the post of Public Information Officer had been vacant for more than a year.
Like other aspects of political life in Washington, D.C., the relationship between the White House press secretary and the media in the Trump era has changed dramatically.
This change came from both sides before McEnnany arrived.
At the White House Correspondent Dinner in April 2018, the comedian Michelle Wolf made a vicious torture of former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders, even MSNBC's host Mika Brezzinski.
The Associated Press White House reporter Jill Colvin asked her at McEnany's first launch, "Can you promise you'll never lie to us from that rostrum?"
Brit Hume, the political analyst for Fox News, is a senior journalist with more than 50 years of experience, who served as Chief White House Correspondent to the American Broadcasting Corporation (ABC) from 1989 to 1996, saying that today's journalists showed “aggression” at press conferences because they despise Trimp.
In this week's Tucker Carlson Tonight show on Fox TV, he said that this was because journalists focused on relativizing with Trump and ignoring the collection of policy news.
“This is not just a cat-and-rat game.” Hume, speaking of the media's intended purpose, says:
“Our aim is not only to keep government officials out of power, but to obtain information.”
Casualization of courthouses by Californian demonstrators
Auckland (California) - Auckland police said that, later on Saturday, peaceful demonstrations in California turned into a clash. The demonstrators set fire to the court, smashed the police station and attacked several police officers.
On Twitter, authorities in Auckland stated that demonstrators had damaged windows, sprayed graffiti and fired fireworks and lasers at police officers.
Some have called for peace on Twitter, with tweets saying that the organizers of the request “help us to provide safe spaces and places for the demonstrators”.
The demonstration began on Saturday night, with the participation of organizations such as the “Mother Wall” (similar to the relevant organizations in Portland, Oregon). At the same time, there was confrontation between the demonstrators and police officers deployed for security in the Federal Court.
Since the 25 May George Floyd incident in Minneapolis, President Donald Trump sent federal police to suppress demonstrations every night.
Initially, the demonstrators demonstrated peacefully in Auckland, but later in the evening they turned violent.
Around 11.30 a.m., the police declared the demonstration “illegally rallies” and demanded that the demonstrators be disbanded.
According to press reports, arson occurred in the Alamida County High Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short period of time.
According to the police, at one point the demonstrators “shattered glass and verbally abused the inhabitants with racial discrimination”.
In a photo posted on Twitter by the relevant authorities, the glass of the police building was smashed and the wall was covered with graffiti.
Cancellation of the visit to Pakistan by senior United Nations officials for flight reasons
Islamabad (Asociación) — The incoming President of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that the visit to Pakistan had been postponed “due to the technical breakdown of the flight”.
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was successfully elected President of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, and was scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he issued a tweet stating that the planned visit to the capital of Pakistan had to be postponed.
The Minister for Foreign Affairs of Pakistan, Shah Mahmood Qureshi, issued a tweet in response to Bozkir, saying that he “welcomes you to visit Pakistan as soon as possible for a constructive and fruitful visit”.
Just one day ago, Qureshi enthusiastically released the news of the visit, expressing the hope that discussions would be held with Bozkir on a range of issues, including Kashmir, between Pakistan and neighbouring India, both of which claim sovereignty over the entire territory of Kashmir.
Since independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both involving the disputed area.
Frequent exchanges of fire between the two sides in Kashmir have resulted in military and civilian casualties.
Pakistan hoped that the United Nations would adopt a resolution allowing those living in the Himalayas to decide whether to integrate or become independent with India or Pakistan, thus contributing to the resolution of the Kashmir issue.
Man United got the Euro crown ticket, Paul Pogba, suggesting he'd stay on the team.
Paul Pogba, the United League player, after the Red Devil locked in the four top seats, suggested that he would stay on the team this summer.
2:0 to take Lester City means that the UC is finally in third place, ahead of Frank Lampard's Whipping Chelsea team.
Before the 12 games, Lester City was still in the lead 14 points from Man United, yet the Reds sang all the way to the Four.
Pogba asked to be released twice in the last two years, so the fans predicted that Pogba would leave the team this summer.
However, the epidemic has meant that very few teams will spend a lot of money this summer, and that no buyer can afford to transfer fees and attract Pogba from old Traford at the same time.
This description has helped Pogba in the last few weeks, and his performance and body language have improved.
After the team won by 2:0, the French player posted a post on Instagram suggesting that he would stay on the team.
Although Pogba may be only celebrating the team ' s requalification, many fans understand that he is strongly suggesting that he will remain on the team with the best players in Europe.
Pogba's current contract is one year away, but the team can be renewed for another year, meaning that he actually has two more years of service.
UCPN-M is eager to extend the Pogba contract and wishes to be clear on his case by the next season.
For Pogba, he was very comfortable on the team, and Bruno Fernandes' alliance gave him more strength.
This Portuguese midfield became the heart of the old Traford, and his arrival allowed Pogba to move long enough into deeper midfield positions and improved his emotions.
Aubameyang helped Arsenal send Walter Ford-Ying down.
Some of Walter Ford's players fell on their knees and others left the field.
Walter Ford's return to his fifth-year-old post just sounded, but no one could say that Walterford had fallen at a time when he needed to pursue Arsenal with all his might.
In fact, it was Walter Ford's most aggressive and powerful game of five years, and, with three minutes behind, Walter Ford almost reversed the battle and finally became unopposed of Arsenal.
At the end of the game, in 16 minutes, Danny Welbeck pushed his heel, and the door pulled Emilio Martínez out. Otherwise, Welbeck would take a third point for Walter Ford, level Arsenal and hold the English Super Seal.
But in the end, Walter Ford failed, and his caretaker coach Hayden Mullins said that he would not have had to work so hard if he had been in the same state as this game.
The whole season, Walter Ford went down, and the boss, Pozzo, managed the team in chaos.
A failed war may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family is unable to lay the foundations for Walter Ford to ensure his return to England.
Walter Ford's frequent change of looks has become an inescapable fate.
In summary, the next lookout will be the 12th time in eight years, but the fresh blood that the lookout can inject is very limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faced a demotion, although he could still take part in the Emirates SuperAllies.
Some players may also choose to leave, such as the attacker Ismaïla Sarr, once again saying that the September championship is unbelievable, but preserving power is the first task at this stage.
Assenauer's coach, Mike Arteta, said that the odds of a match between the two armies were unpredictable, which made the game even more exciting.
Arteta also stated that Walterford's vanguard had been “defendant” and had quickly been replaced by the football term “trying to protect”.
Arsenal said it was not a bad thing, he was ahead of the Arsenal score and Walter Ford had to push for the score.
Walter Ford's opening was missing a ball.
In 40 seconds, Craig Dawson accidentally hit Alexandre Lacazette, sentenced to a penalty of about three minutes after VAR verification, and Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang was hit.
Walter Ford was desperate, Sarr was quick to strike the right goal, Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney were escorting the door.
A former Labour Party member told the Daily Post that they would file a lawsuit even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party.
“I mean, he really, he made the Labour Party competitive again.
He has a long way to go in policy, but he has made an impressive start, and he has done so, allowing many who abandoned the Labour Party and no longer voted for it to re-identify it and to see him as a serious figure.”
Blair does fear that the Labour Party will also be seen as a tax-only party.
He said, “In my experience, if you're a member of the Labour Party, you have to be very careful in tax matters, because the public is not sitting there worried about whether you want to raise taxes, they're more likely to be worried about raising taxes, so you have to be aware of that mentality.”
The new coronary virus will change the world economy forever (view)
As with security measures in the aftermath of the events of 11 September, virus screening may also become a part of our lives.
It is imperative that the necessary infrastructure be invested to respond to future virus testing.
If immunization against the new coronary virus has only a short-term effect, this input can provide security for economic operations.
During the epidemic, many economies implemented policies similar to German “short-time jobs” (Kurzarbeit) subsidies.
Under this policy, the working hours and wages of workers are reduced accordingly, and the underpaid portion is subsidized by the Government.
This initiative maintains the link between business and workers, thus laying a solid foundation for rapid economic recovery.
It was also important to improve the implementation of policies that needed to be a permanent part of the instruments of economic recovery.
Teleworking is likely to become more common.
There is evidence that home-based work can also be as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to accept the working methods of telecommuting.
This approach is likely to continue now that telecommuting has been widely tried and has yielded good results.
As e-commerce has grown, telemedicine, videoconferencing, online teaching and financial technologies have been increasingly adopted, and the crisis has accelerated the digital transition.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains face a variety of shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many of these companies moving production back to the mainland.
According to the Minister of Finance, Fiona Hyslop, the new crown epidemic has had a “catastrophe effect” on activities.
Restrictions to prevent the spread of the new coronary virus have affected such sporting events as festivals and highland sports in Scotland.
Ms. Hyslop said: “Although we have taken a positive step, such as the restoration of car cinemas, most industries have not been operational for some time.
We have worked closely and constructively with industry organizers, who have offered some creative options on how to operate safely in response to the challenges they face.
In addition to direct financial support, part of the funding will be used to promote innovation and sustain the long-term recovery of events.”
Event Industries Advisory Group believes that the industry was the first area to be restricted by the embargo and that it would be one of the areas in which a full lifting of the restrictions could be achieved at the latest.
The Chairman of the Group, Peter Duthie, who also holds the post of Executive Director of the Graggs Securities and Exchange Commission, said that the competition had generated nearly £6 billion annually for Scotland.
He said that “the Government's allocation of additional funds to the industry is necessary and is widely supported by the organizers of the events and by a large number of related supply chain enterprises, which have suffered serious economic losses in the context of the long-standing embargo restrictions”.
Shivraj Singh Chouhan was diagnosed with new coronary pneumonia.
The Chief Minister of the State of Madhya Pradesh, Shivraj Singh Chouhan, confirmed on Saturday that his new coronary virus test was positive and that in his tweets issued on Sunday morning he called himself “all right” and commended the staff working on the front line of the fight against the disease.
The Chief Minister also repeated the warning he had given yesterday calling for compliance with quarantine guidelines, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
“Friends, I'm fine. The anti-epidemic fighters are to be commended for risking their lives and selfless dedication. I salute all the anti-epidemic fighters in the country.” Chouhan posted a tweet at Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, saying that he was admitted to hospital yesterday.
“Keep two metres away, wash your hands hard, wear your mask — this is our most effective weapon against the coronary virus”, he said, “I hope that all people will be able to use these weapons to protect themselves and their families”.
Chouhan concluded by saying, "All of you, be careful, I hope you're all safe and healthy. That's what I pray to God."
Saturday, Chouhan said on Twitter that his virus test was positive, even though he had done his best to avoid it, warning that “a little negligence could have infected the coronary virus”.
On Twitter this morning, Chouhan said that although there was still a threat of infection, there was “no need to be afraid” and called on those with symptoms to take a test.
He said, "If you're infected with the virus, don't be afraid. Don't hide your symptoms, tell the doctor immediately, then start treatment.
There have been nearly 27,000 confirmed cases in Madhya Pradesh, of which 7,600 were active cases and 799 deaths related to the new coronary virus.
India has seen a surge in confirmed cases in the last few weeks, with 45,000 new confirmed cases per day since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of new coronary pneumonia is fast approaching 1.4 million, of which more than 46.7,000 are active cases and more than 32,000 deaths are related to the virus.
So, what's better for studying the rocks on Mars than a rock from Mars?" Professor Caroline Smith, the head of the Museum of Natural History, says.
Smith was also a member of the Mars 2020 scientific team, adding that scientists believed that the rocks they brought back came from Mars.
"The tiny bubbles in the meteorite are exactly the same composition of the atmosphere of Mars, so we're sure that this rock comes from there."
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was created about 600,000 to 700,000 years ago on the impact of an asteroid or comet on Mars, with debris scattered into space.
One of them swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
The meteor, now known as SAU 008, was found in Oman in 1999 and has been in the custody of the Museum of Natural History ever since.
A high-precision laser launcher called Sherloc, which will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Mars rocks and to determine whether they contain organic substances that may indicate that life has existed or still exists on Mars, is part of the instrument installed on the Fortune detector.
Adds a SAU 008 to ensure that the operation is done with maximum accuracy.
Smith adds: "We have chosen this rock in particular because it fits chemically and it's a very hard rock."
"Some of the Mars meteorites we found were fragile, but they were as hard as old boots."
After finding the research-valued rocks, the Fortune will store them on the surface of Mars.
The rocks will then be sent back to Mars for robotic recovery and for research and analysis from space to Earth.
Mambourin, near Veribi, found a man shot.
Last night, a man in western Melbourne was taken to hospital for urgent treatment after being shot.
Upon being called to the police station in Mambourin, near Veribi, at about 8 p.m., the police found a man around 20 years old on Yard Street, suspected of multiple gunshot wounds in the lower half.
Men were taken to hospital for medical treatment and their lives were not in danger.
The police indicated that an investigation was under way into the case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I'm not dressed on Sunday, I'm wearing a domestic dress."
How did your Sunday start?
I stumbled down the stairs and got tripped by dogs on the way to make tea for my wife.
After the ferry came down, we drove on the A3 freeway, went back to London to prepare for the workday, and when we got home, the kids went to bed, and I walked the dog back and ate a bowl of cereal.
I would be happy to return from White Island to London, because I know that if I did not, I would not have a home on White Island.
Arrest of a Kenyan police officer in Garissa in connection with a shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers were arrested on suspicion of having died in a shooting incident.
The shooting occurred on Saturday in Soko Ng'ombe market in Garissa county, east of the country, where protests were under way against a man suspected of murder.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan Police Inspectorate has launched an investigation into the shooting.
The investigation procedure includes an evidentiary analysis of the firearms involved.
Congressman Aden Duale of Garissa called the incident “offensive” and condemned “police violence enforcement” in a post published on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concerns about police enforcement during the epidemic.
According to Amnesty International, 21 people have been killed by the Kenyan police since March, owing to non-compliance with curfew orders and vaccination requirements such as wearing masks.
Early this month, the police dropped tear gas in the capital, Nairobi, and arrested several demonstrators who participated in the annual Saba Saba march, which originated 30 years ago in a protest against former President Daniel arap Moi's dictatorship.
According to former journalists, this year ' s protests became more radical as a result of the repression by the police during the siege.
Security upgrades outside the United States Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security was upgraded outside the United States Consulate General in Chengdu, China, and United States personnel were preparing to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the premises, subjugated the police and plainclothes police, arranged on both sides of the street, where metal fences were erected.
The editor of China's Global Times published a tweet stating that the United States Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e. at 10 a.m. (02000 GMT) on Monday, 27 July.
China accuses some members of the United States Consulate General in Chengdu of “acting in an activity incompatible with his identity”.
The spokesman for the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Wang Wenbin, did not disclose any details, but merely stated that such activities interfered in China ' s internal affairs to the detriment of China ' s security interests.
On Friday, China demanded the closure of the Consulate General of the United States in Chengdu, in response to the request of the United States to close the Consulate General of the Chinese side in Houston, Texas.
Washington demanded that China close the Consulate General in Houston “to protect the intellectual property rights of the United States and the private information of Americans”.
After the closure of the Chinese Consulate General in Houston on Friday, a group of people who looked like United States officials broke into the site from the back door.
On Saturday, Wang Wenbin stated that the act constituted a serious violation of international law and bilateral agreements, and that China would respond without specifying specific measures.
The relative closure of consulates has further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage and human rights issues.
The pranksters used to destroy cars and the baboon in the zoo waved all kinds of tools.
The primates of the Nosley Wildlife Park in Mercesside County are notoriously notorious — they will pounce on cars parked inside the fence, rain scratchers or rear-view mirrors.
The baboons were seen waving knives, knives and even chainsaws at Nosley Wildlife Park in Mercessed County.
Staff fear that pranks will provide weapons to these loathsome primates, who will run towards stopped cars, rain scratchers or rear-view mirrors.
Staff now fear that tourists will provide them with lethal weapons on the grounds of “jokeing”, leading to their increased offensiveness, and some even saw the baboons flip through the toolbox.
A park staff member told the Sunday Times: “We are not sure whether the tourists who wanted to see them attack the cars provided them with weapons or whether they stole them from their pickups and trucks”.
"They do go through other people's toolboxes and walk around with them. Some see baboons dragging a chain saw."
Another staff member said, "We've seen baboons with knives and knives. I wonder if they're being handed over by tourists."
The baboon attack was visible, and even the mechanics in the north-west of England knew about the zoo.
One of the mechanics from Grand Manchester Cerebral said that two of his clients had been severely poisoned by baboons this year, knowing what would happen in the zoo.
They also said that the children wanted to see the monkeys sit in the car the previous minute, and the plates flew away the second minute, but that the problem brought “good business” to the workshop.
The Nosley Zoo maintained that it was as safe as McDonald's, and that the zoo was reopened on 15 June, with security measures in place to deal with the outbreak of the new crown, as long as tourists remained in the car on their way to a 5-mile tour of the zoo.
The zoo, which occupies approximately 222.7 hectares, breeds animals such as lions, tigers, rhinoceros, rhinos and camels, who scorn reports of baboon possession of weapons.
One speaker said, “We believe that many of these stories are exaggerated between mouths and there are signs of fuel and vinegar, leading to the sometimes incredible discovery of objects in the park”.
In 2012, the Modern Company of Automotive Manufacturers placed its new generation i30 car in this zoo for 10 hours with 40 monkeys, thereby proving that the vehicle was strong enough to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United, Chelsea into the Orchard, Bernmouth, Waterford fell out of England.
On Sunday, Man United and Chelsea beat Lester City to the European championship on the last day of the British Super Bowl, while Bernmouth and Waterford were downgraded, ending a five-year race.
After Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard hit the goal, Man United defeated Lester City with a 2:0 score, and Ole Gunnar Solskjaer's team won third.
Chelsea, under Mason Mount's goal and aid, beats the wolf team by 2:0 and ranks fourth, and wins the last seat in the European Cup.
Leicester City will be participating in the European leagues, in addition to the hot thorns, where the team will be levelled against the wolf team at 1:1 in the Crystal Palace and eventually outperformed the wolf team because of its net advantage.
In order to avoid the last-minute match of the two remaining security posts, Aston Villa scored in 84 minutes, was hit one minute later and stayed at 1:1 par with the West Ham Union.
Captain Jack Grealish won an Aston Villa, probably the last game he played on behalf of his childhood club.
Bernmouth beat Everton with a score of 3:1 but eventually one point behind Aston Villa.
Walter Ford downgraded as the second last team after losing to Arsenal at 3:2 p.m.
The League won more than 100 goals during the League, winning a 5:0 victory over the downgraded Novic, and won the right to enter the EU crown, and this game served the Union for 10 years, David Silva, in the last game in England.
Kevin De Bruyne made a record 20 strikes in the season, twice.
Liverpool 3:1 beat the Newcastle League, ended the season of the championship, and the club finally scored 99 points.
In addition, Southampton 3:1 over Sheffield, Danny Ings played 22 times in this season.
And Lester City player Jamie Vardy scored the highest number and won the Golden Boots Award.
Gwyneth Paltro wants the child to have a good "value."
Gwyneth Paltrow stated that she was trying to educate her children by “not just teaching them fish”, hoping that they would build good values.
Paltrow and Chris Martin, the ex-husband, had two children, 16-year-old Apple and 14-year-old Moses, who wanted the children to develop good values rather than relying on the reputation of their parents.
“I am also the reason why I think ... the professional ethics of child-rearing, the importance of children's values to your own values or things that are consistent with their identity,” she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
“The truth is that I can show you the way, but if you just try like others, nothing will happen.”
She added, "What I can do for children, as parents, is to teach them right and wrong and to teach them what to do. I did try to avoid merely giving fish."
Paltro has also taken measures to keep children ' s lives as invisible as possible and to ensure that their social media accounts remain private.
In view of the new elections, the Baath Party eventually agreed to join the coalition Government.
Ted Cruz claims that Pelosi is meant to "solve the problem with money and stop the US."
Washington, Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz accused the Speaker of the House of Representatives, Nancy Pelosi, and the Democratic Party of the House of Representatives of the new crown disease relief programme, which was aimed at “bringing the problem with money and bringing the United States to a standstill”, and consultations on follow-up measures continued.
During a Sunday interview with Face the Nation, Cruz stated that the United States was facing two major crises: the global epidemic claimed more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and the United States was caught in an “absolute economic disaster”.
Cruz criticized Pelosi for “not paying attention to any of these issues” after he gave an interview in The Nation.
Cruz said, "The goal is to solve the problem with money and to stop the United States."
"You need to see, in particular, the $3 trillion bill that she's trying to push, which is purely a blow to money rather than a real solution. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work so that they can feed their families."
The Senate Republican Party and the White House consulted last week on the terms of the fourth phase of the programme to deal with the new crown epidemic, which is expected to reach $1 trillion.
Last weekend, the Chief of Staff of the White House, Mark Meadow, and the Minister of Finance, Steven Mnuchin, returned to Congressional Hill for further discussion with Senate staff, and Mnuchin, in an interview with Fox News on Sunday, said that the Senate majority leader, Mitch McConnell, would reveal the details on Monday.
The most pressing issues facing parliamentarians and the debate between Republicans and Democrats have focused on the fact that the increase in unemployment benefits has expired and will be cancelled this weekend.
The Democratic Party hopes that the “Phase IV” new champion disease relief programme will continue to retain an additional $600 in benefits, while the Republican Party has called for a reduction in that amount, arguing that part of the United States earns more from unemployment than it earns from wages.
Cruz stated that more than half of the United States population currently received increased unemployment benefits that were higher than their earnings while working, and that he had been informed during his talks with small business owners in Texas that some small business owners were also experiencing difficulties in hiring for work.
He said, “Of course they will not come back to work because the federal government sometimes pays them twice as much as their wages.”
Cruz argued that Congress should focus on the adoption of a recovery bill that would ease taxes and regulations on small businesses, thereby enabling the American population to return to work.
He said, “The recovery bill does not knock down trillions of dollars, but instead suspends the tax on wages, which is equivalent to a wage increase for every American working. This way can really get everyone back to work.”
President Trump requested last week that the next bailout include a wage tax reduction, but Mnuchin indicated that the proposal would not be incorporated into the legislative process, as the Republican party did not accept the proposal.
President Trump confirmed that the next bill did not include wage tax cuts, but attempted to blame the Democratic Party.
Cruz challenged the cost of the new programme last week, and on Sunday he expressed his disagreement with the measure proposed by the Republican Party of the Senate.
However, he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed in May by the Democratic-controlled House of Representatives, accusing the other party of deliberately devising a measure that would be detrimental to the interests of the President before the November elections.
He said, "Democrat's only goal is to defeat Donald Trump, who naively thinks that the best way to defeat Donald Trump is to shut down all aspects of America and close every school in America."
Prime Minister Narendra Modi paid tribute to the Qagil hero, saying that Pakistan was trying to “stain a knife behind its back” in India.
Today, on the twenty-first anniversary of the Kargi war, Prime Minister Narendra Modi of India, in tribute to the memory of the martyrs of the armed forces, stated that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan is still trying to “stain a knife behind its back” in India.
Prime Minister Modi stated in his monthly radio talk programme, “Mann ki Baat”, that “Pakistan caused the disaster, and they developed a insidious plan to seize India's land and transfer its internal conflict”.
He said, “Pakistan is brazen in its attempt to occupy Indian land and divert attention from internal unrest”.
“At that time, India was working to restore relations with Pakistan.
But the very essence of what is being called evil is the fight against all without justification.
People of this nature only do evil, even if they want to do good for them.”
He said, “This is why Pakistan ignored India's efforts to maintain relations and stabbed it behind its back.”
He added, "Imagine that the enemy is at the top of the hill, and our army is at the bottom. But our high morale and real power help us win the enemy on the hill."
“Our heroic armed forces have made India show great power in the Kargill World War,” said Prime Minister Modi, while recalling what he saw in his visit to the war zone.
Every year, India celebrates the Kagir victory and commemorates the day the Indian Army took back all the Indian posts in Kagir in the Radak region, which were occupied 21 years ago by the Pakistani army.
On 26 July 1999, the Indian Army announced the successful dismissal of Operation Vijay.
The war in the frozen highlands of Ladak continued for nearly three months, resulting in the death of more than 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier today, several high-ranking officials and army leaders also went to the Memorial to the National War (Amar Jawan Jyoti) to pay tribute to the martyrs, including the Minister of Defence, Rajnath Singh, the Deputy Minister of Defence, Shripad Naik, the Chief of Defence Staff, Bipin Rawat, the Army Chief of Staff, M M. Naravane, the Air Force Commander, RK Shadauria, and Admiral Karambir Singh.
India's relations with Pakistan have been strained by a series of terrorist attacks in the decades since the Kargil war.
India insisted that negotiations and terrorist attacks could not take place simultaneously and that Pakistan needed to dismantle all terrorist camps operating within its territory.
Mortgage agencies warn that SME lending programmes may not be able to achieve their objectives
Small-scale enterprises are often not favoured by large banks and, as a result, are increasingly meeting their financial needs through mesh institutions.
Mr. Callaghan, however, said that there was no advantage for mesh institutions compared to large banks because they could not access the same low-cost funds, and warned that SME guarantee schemes were not welcome.
He said, “I think it is very likely that the programme will not achieve its purpose, and we have been called to expect very cheap funding from us, rather than half the amount of the loan provided by the Government”.
“So, when they called us, they were very disappointed that some people had expressed a desire to get a discount and that the cost of our financing had not changed at all.”
Mr. Callaghan added that such a programme would be “easy” if financial science and technology enterprises were able to access lower-cost financing and then transfer funds to small enterprises.
“This is a bit of a dilemma — large banks have access to funds at a lower cost, but they prefer to avoid risk, so they will not actually lend money in the same way, they will not be able to access data, they will not feel safe unless you have a mortgage on your property”, he said, “Unless we can get cheaper money, I think the market has illusions about the popularity of this programme”.
Moula in Melbourne was also involved in the development of the programme, and the CEO, Aris Allegos, stated that 50 per cent of the proceeds of the guarantees implied in the government programme should be passed on to small businesses, but different lending institutions would take a different approach.
He said, “I can understand that others may not have experienced such a cost structure.”
“This may relate to their financial structure or to the types of loans they make to whom they make loans and how they assess the risks of these categories.”
Mr. Allegos was reluctant to disclose how many loans Moula had specifically granted under the programme, but said that the revised programme “concentrated to reflect the changes that need to be made”, thus bringing greater flexibility to both borrowers and borrowers.
However, he expressed his deep concern about the running “two-speed economy” and its impact on small businesses.
He said, “As far as small businesses are concerned, the biggest problem facing Moula and lending institutions is what you call cliffs and how to manage them.”
“In general, despite a certain increase in demand, it has not yet returned to the level that existed before the outbreak of the new crown.”
Prospa, a network lending agency listed on the Australian Stock Exchange, is also reluctant to disclose its loan lines under the programme, claiming that these are commercially sensitive information, but Chief Executive Officer Greg Moshal said that the Government ' s initiative to expand the programme was welcome.
He said: “We support the enhancements to the programme that enable small enterprises to access a variety of financing products to meet their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.”
Dismantling of the Southern Alliance Monument in the city of Beach, Virginia
Virginia Beach (VJ) - On Sunday, Virginia Beach City removed a 115-year-old South Alliance monument from the public area. Two days ago, the City Council unanimously decided to dismantle it.
According to media reports, the workers demolished the 27-foot (8.2 metres)-high monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was established in 1905 outside the former Princess Anne County Court, where the slave auction was also held.
On Thursday evening, the municipal council unanimously decided to carry out the relevant demolition work.
The City Council heard dozens of speakers who believed that the monument represented a division for many and was constantly reminiscent of the sad past.
In the light of the recent protests and destruction against the other monuments, the monument has now been completely covered and the locked gates have been placed around it.
The memorial will remain for the time being.
The city now needs to await the response of historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to deal with the monument.
Protests continued and the police tried to use new low-lethal tools
In Portland and throughout the country, violent clashes between law enforcement officials and demonstrators have continued and there has been much debate about how to reduce the use of lethal weapons by the police.
Companies are increasing their efforts to provide new high-tech tools such as police remote-controlled handcuffs to replace more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets and flash bullets.
Amnesty International, a human rights organization, analysed 500 videos of protests between 26 May and 5 June and found 125 cases of excessive use of force in 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Amnesty International's weapons investigator Brian Castner stated: “We have seen the misuse of tear gas, pepper spray, pepperball, 40 mm impact bombs, which are essentially a combination of low-lethal weapons in an inappropriate manner”.
Some still maintain the view that selectivity is important to avoid the use of firearms.
Former Deputy Inspector Corey Pegues of the New York Police Department said: “We should provide police officers with all kinds of personal equipment, not with lethal weapons such as guns, because the police are the only occupation in the United States where citizens are empowered to take lives.”
One of the most recent tools, the Bola Wrap, is a hand-held device capable of entangled suspects with an 8-foot-long Kevlar rope.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic equipment (also known as sonic cannons), targeted energy-fired weapons using lasers to heat the skin of humans, new artillery shells made of materials such as foams and white powder, reek bombs and electric stun guns.
In the days following George Floyd ' s killing by the police, the protests turned violent, with the share price of the electric stun manufacturing enterprise Axon rising by more than 18 per cent, a record high.
The total market for low-lethal weapons is expected to reach $11.55 billion by 2023.
Video viewing on new high-tech low-lethal tools used by the police, how to regulate their use and what risks they pose to future policing and communities.
According to the United Nations, there are thousands of armed Pakistani insurgents in Afghanistan.
Islamabad — The United Nations reports that more than 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, most of them illegal Pakistani Taliban groups, responsible for attacking Pakistani military and civilian targets.
According to the report issued this week, the organization known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) is linked to the Afghan branch of the Islamic State (IS).
Some members of TTP even joined the organization affiliated with IS based in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Government of Afghanistan did not respond to the Associated Press request for comments on related issues.
It was reported that IS in Afghanistan (i.e., in the province of Horasan) was hit hard by Afghan security forces and United States and NATO forces, and in some cases by the Afghan Taliban.
The report was prepared by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team, which tracks terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that there are 2,200 members of the Afghan organization IS, which, despite its weakened leadership, continues to consider a Syrian citizen — Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani — as one of its leaders.
The report also mentions information from the Monitoring Group that two senior IS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report states that “IS occupied territory, although declining, remains capable of causing a certain scale of attacks in various parts of the country, including Kabul.
The organization also wants to bring together the Taliban fighters who opposed the agreement with the United States.” The agreement here refers to the peace agreement signed by the United States with the Taliban in February.
The purpose of the agreement was to enable the United States to put an end to its 19-year-old war in Afghanistan and to hope that the Taliban would guarantee that its territory would not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is also expected to ensure that the Taliban are fully engaged in the fight against IS.
The second — and perhaps the most important — part of the agreement calls for talks between the political leaders of the Taliban and Kabul.
Later on Saturday, the United States Department of State issued a statement stating that its Special Envoy for Peace, Zalamay Khalilzad, was still busy in the region, trying to move forward with negotiations that had been repeatedly postponed owing to disputes between the two sides over prisoner release plans.
The United States-backed agreement called on the Afghan Government to release 5,000 Taliban prisoners and, accordingly, the Taliban to release 1,000 government and military personnel as olive branches to be thrown prior to negotiations between the two sides.
Currently, the Government of Afghanistan refuses to release some 600 of these Taliban prisoners and calls them high-profile perpetrators, but proposes to release only the remaining prisoners.
For its part, the Taliban rejected the Government's proposal.
“This is the closest opportunity in history for the two sides to enter into internal negotiations and a key next step towards ending the 40-year-old war in Afghanistan”, the United States Department of State stated,
“Although important progress has been made in the exchange of prisoners, further efforts are needed to resolve the problem.”
Earlier this week, the Taliban political spokesperson stated that the Taliban planned to hold talks with political leaders in Kabul at the end of the month after the end of the Ghor State holiday (Islamic holiday), provided that the release of prisoners was completed.
Pakistan is most concerned about armed elements in Afghanistan, in particular those associated with TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, Lashkar-e-Islam or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched large-scale attacks in southern Sindh and south-west Balochistan during the month.
Several Pakistani soldiers lost their lives in fighting with armed elements in the south-west of Balochistan province.
TTP launched an extremely horrific attack on Pakistan in 2014, which resulted in the death of 140 people at the Pakistan Army Academy.
Most of them are students, some as young as five years old.
The report states that “the presence of terrorist forces outside Pakistan in Afghanistan threatens the security of both countries, with a rough estimate of between 6,000 and 6,500 persons, the majority of whom are members of TTP”.
The largest bank in the United Kingdom, HSBC Bank, denied that China's company, China, had set a trap in the U.S. investigation - RT TV's commercial news.
The Transnational Bank HSBC (HSBC), based in London, indicated through social media that it had not “fabricated evidence” against China, as reported by the Chinese media, leading to the arrest of China as Chief Financial Officer in Canada.
In a now invisible micro-letter article, banks have stated that their actions did not trigger an investigation by the United States into the world’s largest supplier of telecommunications network equipment, as censorship of China’s alleged violation of United States sanctions on Iran began in the second half of 2016, long before the bank intervened.
On Saturday, the bank stated that “the HSBC bank had no hostility towards China and there was no `inflicted' China” and added that it would never distort any facts about the customer.
“In response to the information requested by the United States Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC has merely presented objective facts.
The HSBC Bank has neither `fabricated' evidence nor `concealed' facts.”
The Platform blocked the article, alerting it to “violation of the rules”, which is no longer visible.
HSBC quickly gave this statement after several Chinese media outlets, including newspapers with government backgrounds, accused HSBC of “conspiracy” with Washington to set a trap for China.
In December 2018, at the request of the relevant United States authorities, the Canadian police arrested China as Chief Financial Officer Mon Evening, a company that had been the focus of diplomatic news for a year and a half.
Mon Evening was suspected of using shell companies to violate the sanctions imposed on Iran and to conceal from banks the relationship between China and the Iranian company Skycom, thus facing allegations of fraud.
New evidence cited by the World Times suggests that HSBC had previously been well aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It was reported that the Bank had chosen to “cooperate” with the United States Government in order to escape the harsher penalties imposed on Iran in 2012, when HSBC had been forced to confess to money-laundering and sanctions violations, including a ban on Iran.
At that time, the Bank was ordered to pay a fine of approximately $1.9 billion and measures were needed to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article in response to this incident, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC is well aware of this level of relationship, but deliberately sells confidential business documents from its clients to the United States in order to get the United States Government to ignore the British bank's own misdeeds and avoid the penalties under the 2012 Suspended Prosecution Agreement”.
This newspaper, which is controlled by the Government of China, adds in its public warnings that if it is established that a bank has “collaborative” behaviour, it may be subject to “strict punishment” in China (one of the main markets for HSBC).
In recent weeks, the well-known bank has been blamed on several occasions.
Last month, the HSBC Bank was criticized for supporting the implementation of the new national security law in Hong Kong, a source of more than half of its profits.
HSBC Bank is in a two-faced dilemma: in the West, United States Secretary of State Mike Pompeo called it a “wow-down”; in the East, Chinese media expressed their support for “late arrival”.
Angola: transfer of the Pedalé hospital to the Ministry of Health
On Friday this week, the President of the Republic, João Lourenço, decided to transfer custody of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital, which is under construction in Luanda, to the Ministry of Health.
In a statement, the Office of the President of the Republic stated that the aim was to implement the construction principles of the Government, expand the network of hospitals and ensure better access to medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also mentions that “in the present exceptional circumstances, the measure enables the Angolan people to have a differentiated hospital of reference value equipped with modern technology and sophisticated instruments for the diagnosis and treatment of complex diseases”.
It was stressed that the decision of the President of Angola had also taken into account the country's population growth, which had contributed to the “large demand” for the most diverse and high-quality health services.
The document also states that this need is justified by the fact that hospitals cover the treatment, teaching research and intervention of complex diseases related to ophthalmology and mother and child medicine, areas in which strategies and management are supposed to fall under the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns the loss of a patriot, a determined fighter, in Algeria.
Algiers - The President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed his condolences to Mujahed Mahmoud Guema, a member of the National People's Assembly who had passed away on Friday, and said that Algeria “has lost a patriot and a fighter who has devoted himself to the cause of the people and the motherland”.
President Tebboune wrote in a letter of condolence: “Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama left, Algeria suffered the loss of a patriot, a soldier who had devoted himself to the cause of the people and his country, who had never wavered from the moment he took up his weapons, and he stood firm in his resolve, standing alongside his heroic jihadi brothers, until he took his last breath in this day of grief, and we listened only to the will of the Lord”.
“The late Mahmoud Guema — may he rest in peace — was born in a special generation of patriots full of faith who died for their country.”
“In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp at Wilaya VI, joined the ranks of the revolution, liberated Algeria from the despicable claws of colonialism and gave their lives.”
“The late leader, with a spirit of combat and commitment to building a united and prosperous Algeria, is known by his name.”
The President added: “In the far south, Mahmoud Guema is a well-respected mediator, and once the dissociative sparks have been polished, he remains in it to help our compatriots reach reconciliation.”
“These qualities and values enabled Mahmoud Guema to win the respect and trust of the Tamanrasset people by electing him as a representative of the people for several terms in the National People's Assembly, an ambassador and defender of the concerns and wishes of the people of the region and a voice for all the Algerian people.”
“There is no condolence enough to celebrate Mujahed Mahmoud Guema, and may he rest in peace.
Today, he left us at a time when he needed him most in Algeria”, the President said.
Finally, President Tebboune said, “I pray to God Almighty, that the dead will be bathed in the light of your divine mercy, in heavenless heaven, together with Shuhadaa and the sages, to meet him.
I pray that God will give patience and comfort to his family and his loved ones and that only you will have the power.”
New Crown Disease Travel Guide: Countries imposing immigration restrictions on Bulgarian passengers
Starting this week, the Czech Republic wishes to conduct a new coronary virus test for Bulgarian seasonal workers.
If you visit Bulgaria or Romania within the last 14 days, you must be subjected to mandatory isolation in Italy.
In Austria, compulsory home segregation is required even if the PCR test results are negative. Germany has adopted the same policy with respect to entry personnel.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs has announced that, as of Tuesday, persons arriving in Greece from Bulgaria must present to the Greek border agencies a negative certificate of the new crown virus PCR test, which must be completed within 72 hours prior to entering Greece ' s southern neighbours.
They also declare that a passenger must have a test certificate in order to be allowed to enter the country. The certificate must be issued in English and contains the corresponding three names and numbers on the passenger’s passport or identity card.
The results of the tests are the results of new coronary virus tests conducted at the national reference laboratory, at the national health laboratory or at a private laboratory certified by the relevant national certification authority (not necessarily dedicated to the new coronary virus laboratory).
Greek citizens, citizens resident in Greece and travellers with significant professional reasons are not covered by this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, 28 July, and Wednesday, 29 July, would be tested by the Greek authorities upon arrival if they had not been able to do so prior to departure.
The following requirements remain valid: passengers travelling to Greece by air must complete the electronic Passenger Positioning Form (PLF) at least 24 hours in advance to generate a 2-D code and submit it to the Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For further information, please refer to Travel.gov.gr
At present, there is no change in the requirements for moving to and from the land border between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explains that “passengers who cross the land border into Greece, regardless of their nationality, are required to produce a two-dimensional code at least 24 hours before arrival by filling in the electronic Passenger Positioning Form (PLF).
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda and Makaza checkpoints has led to an increase in traffic along the border near Petrech. There are no problems with transit, there are no truck queues. These people are waiting to sail from Greece.
There are three checkpoints open on the Bulgarian-Greek border. Only this part of Ilinden-Exohi and Ivaylovgrad is currently closed.
Dominic Cummings, visiting SAS base, was taken hostage.
The prime minister's chief adviser experienced the feeling of being held hostage when he visited the base last week.
The idea of holding Dominic Cummings as a hostage with a gun pointed at his head may not be the same for some of the harassed civil servants.
The tough chief adviser to the Prime Minister, when he visited the SAS headquarters line last week, had a first-hand experience.
The Sunday Post revealed that part of his trip on Wednesday included a visit to the “Indoor Counter-Terrorism Training House”, where elite soldiers worked out techniques for dealing with terrorists.
He also led a tour of the Herifud base, inspected the military equipment of the regiment and was briefed on recent operations, including efforts to clear the Islamic State of Iraq terrorists.
Important visitors to the Sterling Line were particularly welcome — to experience the Indoor Counter-Terrorism Training House SAS practiced close-up fighting in this two-story building.
Princess Diana also visited here, and in a training in 1983, her hair was accidentally lit by the spark of a grenade that was dropped.
It is understood that Mr. Cummmings arrived by helicopter and was named after Colonel David Stirling, the founder of the Regiment.
He also visited the adjacent military training area of Petrilas, where several other top-secret units were stationed.
The visit was one of his stops in a highly confidential British security facility, which attracted the attention of politicians and military leaders.
When news of his plans came out earlier this month, Tobias Ellwood, a member of the Conservative Party ' s Queen ' s Bench and Chairman of the House of Commons ' Special Committee on Defence, stated that members of Parliament should be given the same opportunity as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
The Special Committee ' s requests for testimony from special advisers appointed by politicians are rare.
It is understood that SAS senior officials used Wednesday ' s events to highlight the importance of maintaining the Royal Marine Corps and the Airborne Corps.
Accordingly, it has been previously reported that, in order to achieve cost savings, these units, which bring large numbers of personnel to the special forces, are likely to be downsized or merged.
According to a defence source, “SAS consists of 250 soldiers, more than half of whom were previously airborne, and the size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller and may not be sustainable without the support of the Royal Marine Corps”.
“Cummings were advised that these units must be retained in their entirety.”
Mr. Cummings had complained that military procurement “continues to float billions of pounds” and had recently attended meetings at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, as well as planned visits to SBS headquarters in Pool, Dosset County, and to the State's top-secret laboratory in Bolton Town, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defence did not respond to the request for an assessment last night.
Sunday Morning Sun Field: Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
The year 2020 marks the centenary of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart, one of the two most legendary musicals on Broadway.
To pay tribute to the creators of these two songs, Broadway stars Jelani Alladin (Cyclops, Titans) and Christiani Pitts (Golden Kong, Four Seas) and the dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (I Love Red Mother, Shuffle Long) co-executed a modern-style programme based on the original works of Rodgers and Hart, The Wandering Woman, which was also a special recording for the audience of Sunday morning.
The drummer Bryan Carter is the music director and director of the show, and the bass player Russell Hall is on the stage.
Fire control in Larsen County, U.S.A.
Two fires of over 12,141 hectares have broken out in Larsen County, north-east of Sacramento County, California, United States of America, and firefighters are still struggling to rescue them.
According to the California Fire Department, only 40 per cent of the “Gold” mountain fires in the southern part of the Aden area were under control as of Saturday morning, and large numbers of houses and pastures were still threatened by fires, which could have a significant impact on local agriculture, livestock and timber.
The fire continued for four days, burning some 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses and injuring two firefighters.
Firefighters used limited conditions to access and reinforce firelines on a night-to-day basis.
However, weather forecasts indicate that the day was a windy, low-moisture weather.
The “Hog” fire in the west of Susanville has burned down two buildings and some 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Forest and Fire Protection Agency indicated that the fire had been brought under control by firefighters who had accessed and reinforced firelines throughout the night.
By Saturday morning, the fire had lasted six days and 45 percent of the fire had been contained.
Watch out for what looks like a nice gardening trick.
This may be difficult for botanists, but I'm really obsessed with gardening.
On the bright side, I think I am an open-minded or somewhat irrationally optimistic person, and many things in the plant world are often not intuitively judged.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have stepped in numerous pits on such gardening tricks.
While I firmly believe in the value of trying new things, if I am not keen to open the “hell mode”, I can avoid some of the most common gardening tricks I have encountered below.
First, it's probably the biggest minefield — the potato.
In fact, it's an "old-fashioned thing" that someone tried in the 20s.
Tomatoes and potatoes are the same subject, and internal “pipelines” are broadly similar and can indeed create enhanced miracles through marriage.
The problem, however, is that if resource-intensive structures, such as fruit and root crops, are created, large amounts of energy will have to be consumed for metabolism, and the married plants will have to meet the energy needs of both crops simultaneously, and only limited energy will be diverted.
This means that the production of these betrothal plants is very low, unlike what you see in the publicity pictures.
In addition, crops are grown at different times, and the harvesting of potatoes requires the stifling of undeveloped tomatoes.
At the same time, the propaganda picture implies a lot of confusing tricks.
I've seen roses with PS in some catalogues, and some fruit trees produce incredible amounts of fruit.
Some even “lending” photos from my Instagram, falsely claiming to be their test site on the Internet purchase site.
The operation of these few bad businesses has forced you to look at these propaganda images with a skeptical view, especially from the Internet.
If it doesn't look as good as it really is, then it is.
Speaking of blue roses, the blue is relatively rare in the plant world, so if you see a new variety of fancy broomstones, orchids or cactus, with a high concentration of “sterilizers”, that must have been sprayed or dyed.
If the supplier had clearly stated the situation, it would have been nothing, but they rarely would have done so.
The blue butterflies will return to their original white, but the cactus and broomstones will soon fade with an anti-light dye.
Finally, I have to expose the promise to grow a microtree balconies and worse seeds than a balconies.
It's not a plant.
It's a cutting technique that reminds people of ancient trees in the mountains.
The balconies are typically shrubs that have been trimmed and then formed with lines to guide new growth directions.
I knew I'd continue to be obsessed with these gardening tricks, which was one of the pleasures of the experiment.
But avoiding these problems, you may have the opportunity to buy the same kind of material as the seller.
Spanish isolation measures: Do you need segregation in Spain?
No, but if you come back from Spain after 12 o'clock tonight, you have to stay home and separate yourself for 14 days.
The British Attorney General, Humza Yousaf, said: “We decided earlier this week not to impose isolation measures on those who have returned from Spain because the data show an improvement in the spread of the virus.
It is clear, however, that the latest data are of concern to us and that the original decision had to be reversed.
We know that this would be disappointing. But we have made it clear that we will closely monitor the epidemic in all countries and that if the virus re-emerges in one country, we may ask that it be removed from the list of non-segregation measures.”
"The virus is still rampant and deadly. Our first priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread and protect public health."
The Government has not yet made public the rules for British travel to Spain, but if you are prepared to travel to Spain, it would be better to consult the rules for your own region.
The Deputy Director of Emergency Health of Spain, Maria Jose Sierra, said:
"We have a major outbreak here. This could be the second outbreak. We have to focus on the next few weeks."
Pakistan team Imam-Ul-Haq can continue the race after heated injuries.
Pakistan and England will meet as rivals in three test competitions and three T20I competitions.
Pakistani cricket player Imam-ul-Haq was shot and injured in the left hand of a hot-up team in Derby, and the team doctor announced that he was ready to play.
In a statement, the Pakistan Cricket Commission (PCB) stated: “This cricket player, who is accustomed to using his left hand, participated in a warm-up race before the next day's game began”.
On the first day of the game, Imam of the PCB White Team and Naseem Shah of the PCB Green Team were hit in the left hand.
On Friday night, Imam ' s left hand was injured by a ball thrown out of Shah and forced to leave the field, although he participated in the warm-up in the morning and returned to the field on the third day.
More than 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have been diagnosed with a new crown.
Officials from the municipal department and a State-owned bank in Tyrugirabeli, Tamil Nadu, stated that at least 38 employees of one of the main branches of the bank in the city of Tirugirably had a positive test for the coronary virus.
Officials stated that they had advised clients who had been to the branch to volunteer for a virus test.
Prior to that, a senior staff member of the branch had other complications and later died of a new coronary pneumonia.
Officials stated that they had recently conducted a large-scale medical screening of the branch, which had led to the discovery of these positive cases.
The branch leaders indicated that they had completed disinfection and that the bank would either resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officers are contacting customers who have been to the bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second largest state in India with the highest number of confirmed cases in Maharashtra, with a total of 206,737 confirmed cases reported by Tamil Nadu as of Saturday.
The Prime Minister of India will attend the temple foundation ceremony at the disputed site.
New Delhi (UNA) - According to the Trust, which oversees the construction of temples, the Prime Minister of India, Narendra Modi, plans to attend next month's founding ceremony of the Hindu temple at the disputed site in northern India, which was originally built as a 16th century mosque and razed to the ground by Hindu fanatics in 1992.
The ceremony was scheduled to take place on 5 August, which the organizers described as the day for Hindus, but was also the first anniversary of the removal by the Indian Parliament of its sole Muslim-majority semi-autonomous state of Jammu and Kashmir.
Neither supporters nor opponents of the Hindu Nationalist Hindu People ' s Party (BJP), of which Modi is a member, could miss this symbolic day, in its manifesto, in which the party committed itself for decades to the right to self-government in Kashmir and to the construction of a temple on the site of the Mughal-era mosque for Hindu God Ram, which is considered by believers to be the birthplace of Ram.
In view of the fact that the new coronary virus is still spreading throughout India, which is reported to be the third most infected country in the world, the World Hindu Assembly (Vishwa Hidu Parishad), an ally of the Hindu People ' s Party, stated that the ceremony would be broadcast live on national television, limiting the number of people and viewers attending the ceremony.
The spokesperson for the organization, Vinod Bansal, stated at a press conference on Saturday that the temple would be the “centre of timeless social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening Hindu identity”.
Following the overwhelming victory of the Indian People ' s Party in the elections, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favour of the Temple Trust, stating that five acres would be allocated to the Muslim petitioners elsewhere.
Hindu fanatics have long claimed that the Mughal Muslim aggressors built a mosque on the existing temples in the ancient city of Ayodiyah.
In 1992, riots following the demolition of the mosque in December triggered communal violence in which some 2,000 people, mostly Muslims, were killed.
Meanwhile, the Special Court is still seized of the demolition of mosques.
An architect from the hometown of Modi, Ahmedabad, Gujarat, proposed the construction of a temple with a tower sandstone structure at an altitude of 161 feet (49 metres) with five domes.
